Actionable Fixes to Speed Up Your App
​Apply these optimizations in order of impact—the first two alone often turn a sluggish app into an instant one.
​Step 1: Make Sure You Are Not Running a Debug Build
​This is the #1 trap in Rust GUI development. Because egui tessellates thousands of triangles on the CPU every frame, running cargo run (unoptimized debug mode) without a GPU will bring your CPU to its knees.
​Either run with cargo run --release, or add this to your Cargo.toml so even your normal cargo run debug builds compile egui and the renderer at full speed:

[profile.dev.package."*"]
opt-level = 3



Stop Unconditional Repaints
​Check your codebase for ctx.request_repaint().
​If you call ctx.request_repaint() unconditionally inside update(), you are forcing a 60 FPS continuous game loop on a software CPU rasterizer.
​Also beware of animated spinners (ui.spinner()) or continuous progress bars—while visible, they call request_repaint() every frame.
​If you need background data updates, wake the UI thread only when data actually arrives, or use ctx.request_repaint_after(std::time::Duration::from_millis(250)).


Step 3: Turn Off Expensive Tessellation (Feathering & Shadows)
​By default, egui uses feathering—it generates extra microscopic triangle strips around the edges of every shape to fake anti-aliasing—and draws large drop shadows under windows and popups. On a CPU rasterizer, alpha-blending all those extra pixels is very expensive.
​Run this once inside your app's initialization (CreationContext when starting eframe):

pub fn new(cc: &eframe::CreationContext<'_>) -> Self {
    // 1. Disable anti-aliasing feathering (halves vertex/triangle count)
    cc.egui_ctx.tessellation_options_mut(|tess_options| {
        tess_options.feathering = false;
    });

    // 2. Kill window & popup drop shadows (huge fill-rate saver on software renderers)
    cc.egui_ctx.style_mut(|style| {
        style.visuals.window_shadow = egui::epaint::Shadow::NONE;
        style.visuals.popup_shadow = egui::epaint::Shadow::NONE;
    });

    Self { /* your app state */ }
}


Virtualize Long Lists & Large XML/Text Views
​Since you mentioned Oxygen XML Editor, if your app displays large files, trees, or thousands of rows inside a standard egui::ScrollArea::vertical().show(...), egui is laying out and tessellating every single off-screen line on every mouse move.
​Never use .show() for large lists. Always use .show_rows(), which only processes the exact rows currently visible in the viewport:


let row_height = ui.text_style_height(&egui::TextStyle::Body);
let total_rows = my_items.len();

egui::ScrollArea::vertical().show_rows(
    ui,
    row_height,
    total_rows,
    |ui, row_range| {
        for row_index in row_range {
            ui.label(&my_items[row_index]);
        }
    },
);


